365
00:32:02,504 --> 00:32:11,990
[SPEAKER_01]: But if your sense of self is concerned about no hostility, no ill will, and it sticks to that, then eventually becomes redundant, and the result of that is the experience of loving kindness.

366
00:32:12,889 --> 00:32:22,697
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can intend it, you can, whatever, build on top of that, go further, and it all eventually results in equanimity, immovable.

367
00:32:25,439 --> 00:32:26,420
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't do that.

368
00:32:26,540 --> 00:32:26,960
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't.

369
00:32:27,581 --> 00:32:30,203
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, yeah, that's why it's quite...

370
00:32:30,939 --> 00:32:37,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's quite dangerous anyway, because people read Brahmavihārās and you think, oh, now I just have to start thinking positive thoughts, good thoughts.

371
00:32:37,862 --> 00:32:38,463
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine.

372
00:32:38,523 --> 00:32:39,123
[SPEAKER_01]: You can think that.

373
00:32:39,163 --> 00:32:46,106
[SPEAKER_01]: But don't think that that is the practice of Brahmavihārās, because then you will never make the effort to find out what the actual practice of Brahmavihārās really is.

374
00:32:47,107 --> 00:32:50,849
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can know that whatever you're doing is not the practice of Brahmavihārās, because you're not an ārahant.

375
00:32:52,369 --> 00:32:53,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were to do it,

376
00:32:54,658 --> 00:33:09,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Not even that long, it would result, if you don't do it correctly, it would result in destruction of all fetters, because it undermines the very framework of the sense of self, in regard to me, in regard to others, in regard to both, in regard to neither.

377
00:33:10,588 --> 00:33:12,109
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no room for self in them.

378
00:33:12,989 --> 00:33:23,176
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, there's no room for self in jhānas, which is why brahmicāras can also be used to then establish a mind into a jhāna upon them, depending how you start intending to things.

379
00:33:29,873 --> 00:33:34,496
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize without his mind, without hostility and without ill-will.

380
00:33:35,837 --> 00:33:41,440
[SPEAKER_00]: And as you're saying, ill-will is... non-ill-will is a greater phenomenon.

381
00:33:41,700 --> 00:33:41,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

382
00:33:43,261 --> 00:33:46,283
[SPEAKER_00]: And ill-will is much smaller, much more particular.

383
00:33:46,303 --> 00:33:47,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it needs to be particular.

384
00:33:47,584 --> 00:33:49,965
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have ill-will in the general manner.

385
00:33:50,566 --> 00:33:55,249
[SPEAKER_01]: Because even then, if you're like generally bad mood, well, that becomes a particular thing.

386
00:33:55,729 --> 00:33:57,530
[SPEAKER_01]: So, ill-will is always directional.

387
00:33:58,588 --> 00:34:00,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that, non-near-will is always greater.

388
00:34:01,570 --> 00:34:04,592
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always the basis of it.

389
00:34:04,612 --> 00:34:22,526
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if someone was... someone was abiding, most of the time, in non-near-will, or attending to that phenomenon, and they get angry, but they can't really get angry that much... No, no, no.

390
00:34:23,727 --> 00:34:27,990
[SPEAKER_01]: No, the displeasing, the disagreeable circumstances, if their mind is established,

391
00:34:29,104 --> 00:34:34,828
[SPEAKER_01]: If they dwell often, as the Buddha said, you should dwell often in these states, then they will kind of rise.

392
00:34:34,948 --> 00:34:37,130
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically like like heating the pan.

393
00:34:38,031 --> 00:34:41,573
[SPEAKER_01]: If the pan is just mildly warm, when you throw the water, well, the water will linger.

394
00:34:42,274 --> 00:34:46,837
[SPEAKER_01]: But imagine you keep warming up that pan for quite some time and it's red hot.

395
00:34:47,678 --> 00:34:52,601
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how much water you chuck on it, it will just immediately evaporate.

396
00:34:52,642 --> 00:34:52,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Go.

397
00:34:53,622 --> 00:34:54,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Wouldn't linger at all.

398
00:34:57,748 --> 00:35:07,116
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you know, you get careless, the fire drops down, cools down, then even a tiny drop of water will linger a long time, if it's a cold pan.

399
00:35:08,918 --> 00:35:09,899
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same with the mind.

400
00:35:11,300 --> 00:35:22,870
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mind doesn't dwell upon, like, the phenomena, the notions, the abstractions of non-ill-will, just think abstractly what it is to have non-malice and non-hostility.

401
00:35:23,791 --> 00:35:24,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Dwell on that long enough.

402
00:35:26,158 --> 00:35:29,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't actively try to love people or anything like that.

403
00:35:29,239 --> 00:35:32,040
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already particularizing of that thing, which is a mistake.

404
00:35:33,461 --> 00:35:33,621
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

405
00:35:33,641 --> 00:35:38,883
[SPEAKER_01]: The result of non-hostility, non-ill-will, will be a genuine loving-kindness.

406
00:35:39,003 --> 00:35:44,926
[SPEAKER_01]: A loving-kindness that doesn't induce the sense of self, that doesn't induce the sense of conceit.

407
00:35:45,406 --> 00:35:49,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Loving-kindness is just a pure framework of me and others.

408
00:35:49,688 --> 00:35:52,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Neither of us can really own what we think we own.

409
00:35:52,649 --> 00:35:55,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you just have loving-kindness and compassion.

410
00:35:57,725 --> 00:36:02,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Compassion to the kind of indifferent framework of your experience as it is.

411
00:36:04,587 --> 00:36:10,168
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't, or rather, you can get that directly through this indirect practice.

412
00:36:11,568 --> 00:36:16,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is the, in a way, in itself, on its own, it's a direct way, so to speak.

413
00:36:16,730 --> 00:36:20,010
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not a direct way from what people think it's a direct way.

414
00:36:20,570 --> 00:36:22,531
[SPEAKER_01]: I am spreading beams of loving-kindness.

415
00:36:22,691 --> 00:36:23,951
[SPEAKER_01]: There's never been a point enough.

416
00:36:24,591 --> 00:36:26,952
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you are radiating beams of loving-kindness,

417
00:36:28,147 --> 00:36:31,129
[SPEAKER_01]: What would you do then, when it comes to upāyaka?

418
00:36:31,609 --> 00:36:33,490
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you radiate beams of indifference?

419
00:36:33,830 --> 00:36:34,510
[SPEAKER_01]: How does that work?

420
00:36:35,331 --> 00:36:38,172
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sending you the beams of my equanimity.

421
00:36:38,973 --> 00:36:41,894
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm moving these beams towards you of this immovability.

422
00:36:42,815 --> 00:36:43,635
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just nonsense.

423
00:36:47,657 --> 00:36:52,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's always a painful practice.

424
00:36:53,300 --> 00:36:53,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Contrived.

425
00:36:54,321 --> 00:36:55,702
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a very contrived practice.

426
00:36:58,037 --> 00:37:01,638
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't always seem inadequate, really.

427
00:37:01,838 --> 00:37:02,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course.

428
00:37:03,119 --> 00:37:04,920
[SPEAKER_01]: It's very adequate if it's done rightly.

429
00:37:05,200 --> 00:37:05,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

430
00:37:06,120 --> 00:37:06,960
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean...

431
00:37:07,040 --> 00:37:11,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Almost effortless.

432
00:37:12,342 --> 00:37:18,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Because all you have to do is pay attention and make sure non-ill will, non-hostility prevails.

433
00:37:20,826 --> 00:37:24,347
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you have to do is guard the gates, so to speak.

434
00:37:26,383 --> 00:37:31,827
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more fortified those gates are, the less things will be going through of ill-will and hostility.

435
00:37:32,728 --> 00:37:37,031
[SPEAKER_01]: And when that becomes a state you abide in, often enough, that's your brahmavihāda.

436
00:37:37,391 --> 00:37:38,412
[SPEAKER_01]: The result is metta.

437
00:37:42,875 --> 00:37:46,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Mudita, karuṇa, and then just complete, immovable, indriyātula.

438
00:37:48,659 --> 00:37:49,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

439
00:37:52,221 --> 00:37:55,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Because metta, mudita, karuṇa are the subtle movements.

440
00:37:57,243 --> 00:38:07,693
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're so subtle, so neutral, that any of them can be a base for the full destruction of fetters and other energy, which then results in equanimity.

441
00:38:13,599 --> 00:38:22,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Inasmuch, you need only one first jhāna to dwell in it sufficiently enough, abide in it, and then all the fetters will fall off and you'll be all right.

442
00:38:26,278 --> 00:38:28,099
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need the actual jhāna.

443
00:38:28,479 --> 00:38:31,660
[SPEAKER_01]: You need the strength of the mind that pertains to the actual first jhāna.

444
00:38:32,840 --> 00:38:34,820
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can have different means of practice.

445
00:38:34,840 --> 00:38:39,842
[SPEAKER_01]: You can arrive at adhārāṇaship through bhagavad-gītā, even if you never thought about or heard about jhānas.

446
00:38:41,502 --> 00:38:45,703
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just a form of establishment of your mind, a way you can establish your mind.

447
00:38:47,755 --> 00:38:59,898
[SPEAKER_01]: But when Buddha says, oh, you need the first jhāna as a basis for the destruction of things, means you need that strength of mind that corresponds to the first jhāna, which would correspond, you know, to various other things as well, but they were talking about jhānas at the time.

448
00:39:17,337 --> 00:39:32,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it does seem to suggest, in the sūtra as well, that if you're... if you are... if there's defilements, you can still establish your mind on these... on nānyā world, but you might have other defilements coming up.

449
00:39:33,241 --> 00:39:33,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

450
00:39:34,621 --> 00:39:36,221
[SPEAKER_00]: But your mind still can be established.

451
00:39:36,541 --> 00:39:36,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

452
00:39:37,702 --> 00:39:43,943
[SPEAKER_01]: And the better you get, the more... the more established it gets, the less lingering defilements will be.

453
00:39:46,213 --> 00:39:57,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and he's obviously talking about... if someone's doing that and they got... it's like the... he gave you born into a... what is it... pure birds of pure... Yeah, quite subtle realms.

454
00:39:57,602 --> 00:39:57,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

455
00:39:58,162 --> 00:40:01,285
[SPEAKER_00]: They've got very mild radiance or the pure radiance.

456
00:40:01,305 --> 00:40:02,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

457
00:40:02,246 --> 00:40:04,367
[SPEAKER_00]: They're measurable radiance or limited radiance.

458
00:40:04,868 --> 00:40:05,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

459
00:40:05,028 --> 00:40:06,289
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it depends on how strong you are.

460
00:40:06,429 --> 00:40:09,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Depends on how often your mind was abiding in those refined... well... Yeah.

461
00:40:09,872 --> 00:40:11,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Relatively refined states.

462
00:40:11,353 --> 00:40:13,074
[SPEAKER_01]: They're refined from the practitioner's point of view.

463
00:40:14,728 --> 00:40:19,792
[SPEAKER_01]: But, eh... yeah, if your mind starts abiding in non-facilitating, non-ill will get established upon brahma-vihāras.

464
00:40:20,212 --> 00:40:21,053
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your abiding.

465
00:40:21,073 --> 00:40:21,934
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your state.

466
00:40:21,974 --> 00:40:22,814
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing...

467
00:40:22,834 --> 00:40:30,981
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you still perceive, oh, this is more refined than what I used to do, what I used to think, what I used to feel, eh... but now it's the state that you have.

468
00:40:31,121 --> 00:40:39,607
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you can recognize, oh, there is... You know, when the Sariputta thinks about jhānas and says, it gets established in this and it's great and mindfulness... Is there more?

469
00:40:39,767 --> 00:40:40,288
[SPEAKER_01]: There is more.

470
00:40:45,478 --> 00:40:52,328
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as you get the principle out of it, fully develop it, and you'll be an Arhat, then you can go wherever you want to go or not.

471
00:40:56,033 --> 00:40:59,718
[SPEAKER_01]: But if somebody doesn't become an Arhat, but abides in those states, then the Buddha did say,

472
00:41:02,193 --> 00:41:15,221
[SPEAKER_01]: These realms of rebirth correspond to abiding in those mental states, which is consequently abiding in the mental states that are infused with lust, aversion, delusion.

473
00:41:15,801 --> 00:41:19,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what corresponds to that is the annular realm, if you're lucky.

474
00:41:22,105 --> 00:41:26,407
[SPEAKER_00]: If I'm going to read this, God actually could be reborn in these god realms.

475
00:41:28,409 --> 00:41:28,969
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of...

476
00:41:31,148 --> 00:41:33,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Also, unfortunately, you never think of...

477
00:41:33,210 --> 00:41:34,210
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still a duration.

478
00:41:34,310 --> 00:41:35,151
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still enduring.

479
00:41:35,351 --> 00:41:38,554
[SPEAKER_01]: It's pleasurable, but it's still... you have to go through with it.

480
00:41:40,395 --> 00:41:41,356
[SPEAKER_01]: And it still comes to an end.

481
00:41:44,398 --> 00:41:52,144
[SPEAKER_00]: I wonder if your... your efforts towards... arahantship is wasted.

482
00:41:52,865 --> 00:41:53,285
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean?

483
00:41:53,485 --> 00:41:54,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you get reborn in this karma.

484
00:41:54,926 --> 00:41:55,607
[SPEAKER_01]: No, of course not.

485
00:41:57,120 --> 00:42:01,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Your efforts to nibbāna cannot possibly waste it.

486
00:42:01,745 --> 00:42:09,513
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you become... like that's how sometimes people over a course of lifetimes build up enough and then they become arahants.

487
00:42:10,033 --> 00:42:14,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Because all the efforts from previous lives, toil and up always, nothing can go to waste.

488
00:42:15,338 --> 00:42:16,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, sensuality goes to waste.

489
00:42:17,441 --> 00:42:18,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Your will goes to waste.

490
00:42:18,682 --> 00:42:19,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance goes to waste.

491
00:42:21,208 --> 00:42:24,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Any effort made to uproot those cannot possibly go to waste.

492
00:42:24,650 --> 00:42:30,354
[SPEAKER_01]: You might not get the immediate result, but it's like, again, like a drop, drop, drop in the ocean.

493
00:42:30,454 --> 00:42:33,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Bit by bit by bit by bit by bit, you fill up the entire ocean.

494
00:42:39,881 --> 00:42:50,288
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what usually happens, based on the suttas, when people, they pass away, but they met the previous Buddha, and they obviously got inspired, made offerings,

495
00:42:51,496 --> 00:42:53,937
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, it seems like great, man.

496
00:42:53,977 --> 00:43:04,600
[SPEAKER_01]: You get all this pleasure and then become an arahant.

497
00:43:05,801 --> 00:43:05,921
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

498
00:43:05,981 --> 00:43:10,302
[SPEAKER_01]: The greatest pleasure is to become an arahant.

499
00:43:10,382 --> 00:43:15,184
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just you're delaying your pleasure through being reborn in these celestial realms.

500
00:43:15,204 --> 00:43:17,264
[SPEAKER_01]: They can last for years.

501
00:43:17,304 --> 00:43:18,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Goodness me!

502
00:43:18,085 --> 00:43:18,705
[SPEAKER_01]: For years!

503
00:43:21,315 --> 00:43:23,417
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... you don't know what you're doing.

504
00:43:26,259 --> 00:43:27,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Better than nothing is.

505
00:43:27,780 --> 00:43:28,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

506
00:43:28,080 --> 00:43:28,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Better than nothing.

507
00:43:28,861 --> 00:43:29,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Better than animal realm.

508
00:43:30,742 --> 00:43:31,182
[SPEAKER_01]: But still.

509
00:43:31,703 --> 00:43:36,326
[SPEAKER_01]: But if the efforts were genuinely aiming at nibbāna, that cannot go to waste.

510
00:43:36,526 --> 00:43:37,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

511
00:43:38,368 --> 00:43:39,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

512
00:43:39,829 --> 00:43:40,670
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it would have meant.

513
00:43:41,050 --> 00:43:48,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you cultivate the right view for a finger snap, if you cultivate loving-kindness for a finger snap, that's how you make streams of merit.

514
00:43:50,172 --> 00:43:54,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Because merit pertains to the future benefits of your present action.

515
00:43:55,816 --> 00:44:01,741
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through applying yourself... Like, you make merit through giving and offering.

516
00:44:02,441 --> 00:44:10,247
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's nothing in comparison to merit that you get through having a finger snap of the right view.

517
00:44:11,668 --> 00:44:13,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Finger snap of jhāna.

518
00:44:13,950 --> 00:44:16,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Finger snap of brahmavihāyas.

519
00:44:18,359 --> 00:44:21,842
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to, you know, you need to have the finger snap of the right thing.

520
00:44:22,182 --> 00:44:22,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

521
00:44:23,023 --> 00:44:30,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I want to qualify that, because then, you know, you do have people as well, as long as I'm trying, I'll make merit and I'll be fine.

522
00:44:30,450 --> 00:44:31,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Not necessarily.

523
00:44:31,170 --> 00:44:40,098
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're trying the wrong thing, if you're following the wrong teachings, if you're following things that cannot lead to understanding, well, you don't have the finger snap, even a finger snap of the right view.

524
00:44:43,216 --> 00:44:53,104
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not easy, but if it's done, even those fleeting moments of the right view of this stream of magic, which means, yeah, exactly that, in the future, lots of benefits from it.

525
00:44:55,766 --> 00:44:59,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Someone could say that most people abide in non-real world.

526
00:45:01,230 --> 00:45:02,111
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but not...

527
00:45:02,131 --> 00:45:03,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Most of the time I'm upset a bit.

528
00:45:03,780 --> 00:45:05,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, right, except when I'm not.

529
00:45:05,121 --> 00:45:05,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Except when I'm not.

530
00:45:05,922 --> 00:45:09,044
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you know... Oh, I keep the precepts, except when I don't.

531
00:45:09,665 --> 00:45:14,609
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's nothing in comparison to the time I spend keeping the precepts, thus my precepts are much greater.

532
00:45:15,129 --> 00:45:15,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

533
00:45:16,330 --> 00:45:16,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

534
00:45:16,771 --> 00:45:17,812
[SPEAKER_00]: My mind is not...

535
00:45:17,852 --> 00:45:18,652
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not getting angry or anything.

536
00:45:18,672 --> 00:45:24,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but there is a difference in... in knowing libido in your will and being delusional about it.

537
00:45:26,039 --> 00:45:26,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing.

538
00:45:26,799 --> 00:45:27,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing, exactly.

539
00:45:29,327 --> 00:45:31,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you know what the phenomenon of ill-will is?

540
00:45:31,569 --> 00:45:31,949
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't.

541
00:45:31,989 --> 00:45:33,690
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you possibly be abiding in it?

542
00:45:34,070 --> 00:45:34,471
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not.

543
00:45:34,591 --> 00:45:36,512
[SPEAKER_01]: You're abiding in delusion, exactly.

544
00:45:36,732 --> 00:45:38,874
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your mind is where your ill-will is.

545
00:45:38,914 --> 00:45:40,715
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that that ill-will is not always present.

546
00:45:40,975 --> 00:45:46,779
[SPEAKER_01]: But delusion certainly is, which is why, on the basis of that delusion, you can have ill-will arising, you can have hostility, lust.

547
00:45:47,200 --> 00:45:49,101
